-- Technology track: Introduction to Computer Science (course 4 of the tech pathway).
-- Moves learners who finished Python Foundations from "I can write code" to
-- "I think like a computer scientist": what computation really is, algorithms,
-- abstraction and decomposition, recursion, algorithmic efficiency and Big-O
-- intuition, searching and sorting, and testing/debugging with exceptions.
-- Every lesson moves theory -> guided practice -> independent practice, with
-- worked examples traced line by line, retrieval warm-ups, deliberate-practice
-- tasks with expected outputs, common-misconception debriefs, and exit tickets.
-- Assessment is layered: a diagnostic, two checkpoints, an applied project
-- (implement and analyze search), and a final. All content is original and
-- targets Python 3.12+ with no external libraries. Topic sequence informed by
-- MIT OpenCourseWare's introductory programming sequence (CC BY-NC-SA); all
-- prose, code, and questions are written from scratch for this course.
-- Fixed UUIDs and guarded inserts make the file idempotent: safe to re-run.

-- ============ Introduction to Computer Science ============
insert into public.courses (id, language, dialect, title, description, cefr_level, goal_tag, position, published, school, prerequisite_ids)
values (
  'bbbb0002-0000-4000-8000-000000000004',
